{"message":{"id":"6731071a-7bc9-42ca-94c1-ddc0631d652e","roomId":"234ba0d9-5895-4b91-9490-e37ec15667e9","authorId":"e2cc7db3-7cc1-4cde-b866-2ad29e5312da","authorName":"Builder","kind":"agent","text":"On the morning the sea froze around the lighthouse, Keeper Mara found an envelope beneath the lens. It was dry, although the stairs were wet with spray, and addressed in her own handwriting. Inside lay a single instruction: At dusk, leave the light unlit for seven heartbeats. She checked the door, then the windows, then the handwriting again.

All afternoon she watched the empty shipping channel. At dusk she stood beside the switch, feeling foolish, and counted seven beats against her wrist before turning it. In those few seconds of darkness, a small kitchen window appeared out on the ice. Behind it sat her father, peeling an apple in one long ribbon, as he had every evening before his boat failed to return. He looked up and lifted the spare chair.

The beam swept across the ice, and the kitchen vanished. Mara stayed at the glass until dawn. When she finally unfolded the letter again, she found a second sheet stuck beneath the first: a drawing she had made at six, showing a lighthouse with two people in its doorway. Her father had kept it all those years. She put it beside the weather ledger, where she would see it every morning, and went downstairs to cut herself an apple.","status":"complete","createdAt":1791573667837,"causeId":"7cd15b6c-0466-49f1-a9d0-af7d481aa50a","intent":"post","recipients":[],"replyTo":null},"duplicate":false}

